{
  "schema_version": "howl.exploration_result/v1",
  "exploration_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261",
  "parent_request_id": "candidate-17bf3227bca9",
  "objective": "Challenge this selected opportunity and its experiment design (evidence id design-v1) using the measured feasibility facts (evidence id s1-feasibility). Attack: whether the November pool decision really changes with a ranking; whether the outcome definition (>=50 MLB PA or >=20 IP next season) measures value or mostly opportunity; leakage or hindsight in features, splits or outcome; fairness and strength of baselines B0/B1; whether k=50 and the +3 hits threshold are justified; base-rate and sample-size risk; what existing public tools already do this; and above all: what would make this project LOOK useful while actually providing little or no baseball advantage. Use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN for missing evidence, CITE for supplied evidence ids, and IDEA only for a concrete design correction.",
  "originating_component": "howldream",
  "authority": {
    "type": "ADVISORY",
    "executable": false
  },
  "candidates": [
    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/0",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261",
      "parent_request_id": "candidate-17bf3227bca9",
      "objective": "Challenge this selected opportunity and its experiment design (evidence id design-v1) using the measured feasibility facts (evidence id s1-feasibility). Attack: whether the November pool decision really changes with a ranking; whether the outcome definition (>=50 MLB PA or >=20 IP next season) measures value or mostly opportunity; leakage or hindsight in features, splits or outcome; fairness and strength of baselines B0/B1; whether k=50 and the +3 hits threshold are justified; base-rate and sample-size risk; what existing public tools already do this; and above all: what would make this project LOOK useful while actually providing little or no baseball advantage. Use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN for missing evidence, CITE for supplied evidence ids, and IDEA only for a concrete design correction.",
      "text": "CITE: design-v1\nCITE: s1-feasibility\nFACT: milbfa_2018_events=585\nFACT: milbfa_2025_events=839\nFACT: method_disagreement_2018_minor=530 vs 417\nCALC: 530 - 417 = 113\nCONFLICT: method_disagreement_2018_minor\nASSUMPTION: The November decision may not change with a ranking. Most of the several hundred electors are low-level minor leaguers a club would never pursue, and clubs likely already shortlist from internal reports, so a whole-pool ranking may only reorder players that staff would have reviewed anyway.\nASSUMPTION: The outcome (>=50 PA or >=20 IP) mostly measures opportunity. It depends on the signing club's roster holes, injuries, and 40-man status, so it can reward models that predict who gets chances (age, MLB time in Y) rather than who is good. A rate-based or WAR-above-replacement-style outcome, or a regression on playing time, would separate the two.\nASSUMPTION: Prior MLB playing time is both a feature and a near-proxy for the outcome, so M may beat B1 simply by rediscovering B0. B1 omits MLB playing time, which makes it a weakened comparator. The design says the better of B0/B1 is the benchmark, but a stronger baseline (B0 and B1 combined, or a logistic regression on only 2-3 features) is missing.\nASSUMPTION: Public tools already cover parts of this: public projection systems, minor-league leaderboards, transaction trackers, and prospect-ranking sites. The design lists no comparison against them, so any win over weak baselines may say nothing about added value.\nASSUMPTION: Leakage risk: the \"elected free agency\" event can embed selection (players the club chose not to retain), and the Dec 31 availability cutoff is hard to verify for stats that get revised or for retroactively corrected transaction records. Players repeating across cohorts also carry label-correlated history into training and holdout.\nASSUMPTION: Hindsight risk: the 2025 holdout is described as uninspected, but the designers know the pool size and the 2026 season is finished. Cohort definitions, k, and the +3 threshold were set by people who may know aggregate base rates, so the pre-registration is only partially blind.\nASSUMPTION: k=50 is justified only by a hypothesis about club signing volume that is unchecked. A club does not choose from a fixed 50, and the pool has a large share of already-ineligible or unreachable players. Top-50 hits could also be dominated by players with recent MLB time who were never gettable cheaply.\nASSUMPTION: The +3 hits threshold has no derivation from variance, the base rate, or the value of a depth player. With a single holdout cohort and possibly 15-60 positives, bootstrap noise on top-50 counts may be about as large as 3. Passing could be luck, and failing could hide a real gain.\nASSUMPTION: Cohort-level sample size is small: roughly six training cohorts and one holdout, with correlated players across years. The design's early-stop at <15 positives per cohort is low, since a top-50 comparison at that level is almost uninformative.\nASSUMPTION: The pool is uncertain because the minor-league versus MLB election split is unresolved (113 players apart in 2018). Using the whole pool avoids that issue, but mixes populations with very different base rates, so AUROC may look strong from easy separation (MLB veterans vs. A-ball players) while top-50 within the relevant segment shows nothing.\nASSUMPTION: The project would look useful but give little advantage if: AUROC is high due to easy cases; hits are driven by players who re-sign quickly with their own club; the headline metric counts players clubs could not acquire; the Cubs retrospective is read as validation; or the model is compared to nothing a real scout uses. Private club data would likely already cover the same ground.\nASSUMPTION: Dropping 2020 and scaling the 2019 cohort's threshold by 60/162 introduces arbitrary choices that can be tuned after the fact. The 2021 cohort follows a disrupted minor-league return and may differ in structure.\nASSUMPTION: Source terms limit use to individual, non-commercial, non-bulk, which may conflict with building multi-year cohort tables across hundreds of players per year.\nUNKNOWN: outcome_positive_base_rate_by_cohort\nUNKNOWN: share_of_pool_with_prior_MLB_time\nUNKNOWN: public_tool_comparison_results\nUNKNOWN: typical_club_minor_league_contract_and_NRI_volume\nUNKNOWN: bootstrap_variance_of_top50_hit_difference\nUNKNOWN: mlb_stats_api_terms_compatibility_with_cohort_building\nIDEA: Replace the single +3 rule with a pre-registered threshold derived from a simulation of bootstrap variance on the validation cohort, and report effect sizes across all cohorts via rolling-origin evaluation instead of one holdout.\nIDEA: Stratify the pool before ranking (prior MLB time vs. none, pitcher vs. hitter), evaluate top-k within the no-MLB stratum, and use a rate-based or above-replacement outcome alongside the playing-time threshold.\nIDEA: Add stronger baselines: B0+B1 combined, last-year-stats-only logistic, and a public projection system, plus a \"tops by age only\" null. Report an opportunity-adjusted outcome that conditions on the signing club's roster openings where public.\nIDEA: Choose k from measured public signing counts (Cubs and other clubs) before looking at any holdout outcomes, and show a sweep over k=25/50/100 with intervals.",
      "condition": "dream",
      "trust": "UNVERIFIED",
      "status": "UNRESOLVED",
