    {
      "schema_version": "howl.candidate/v1",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "source_run_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261",
      "parent_request_id": "candidate-17bf3227bca9",
      "objective": "Challenge this selected opportunity and its experiment design (evidence id design-v1) using the measured feasibility facts (evidence id s1-feasibility). Attack: whether the November pool decision really changes with a ranking; whether the outcome definition (>=50 MLB PA or >=20 IP next season) measures value or mostly opportunity; leakage or hindsight in features, splits or outcome; fairness and strength of baselines B0/B1; whether k=50 and the +3 hits threshold are justified; base-rate and sample-size risk; what existing public tools already do this; and above all: what would make this project LOOK useful while actually providing little or no baseball advantage. Use ASSUMPTION lines for challenges, UNKNOWN for missing evidence, CITE for supplied evidence ids, and IDEA only for a concrete design correction.",
      "text": "CITE: design-v1\nCITE: s1-feasibility\nFACT: milbfa_2018_events=585\nFACT: milbfa_2025_events=839\nFACT: method_disagreement_2018_minor=530 vs 417\nCALC: 530 - 417 = 113\nCONFLICT: milb_vs_mlb_separation_2018\nASSUMPTION: The design avoids the S1 separation conflict by ranking the whole pool, but that makes the pool include players the decision-maker would never pursue (MLB-service-time veterans, 6-year minor-league free agents, players already agreed to re-sign). Top-50 hits may then be dominated by obvious MLB-experienced players, which B0 already ranks highly; M's lead over B0 could come from re-discovering \"had MLB time in Y\" rather than finding anything a scout would not already see.\nASSUMPTION: The November decision may not change with a ranking. Clubs already have internal lists, and the best players sign within days, often with the club that holds their rights or on terms agreed before the pool opens. The design provides no evidence that a club's actual choice set at decision time resembles the full elected-free-agency list.\nUNKNOWN: fraction_of_pool_that_is_actually_signable_and_available_to_a_given_club\nASSUMPTION: The outcome (>=50 PA or >=20 IP in Y+1) measures opportunity at least as much as value. A player who signs with a team lacking depth reaches 50 PA through roster need, and a good player blocked on a deep roster does not. The design admits this bias but proposes no correction. It would also reward predicting \"who gets a 40-man spot\", which can be learned from MLB time and level alone.\nIDEA: Add a value-conditioned secondary outcome (e.g., WAR or runs above replacement per PA/IP, with a minimum-opportunity gate) and a opportunity-adjusted outcome (e.g., stratify by signing club's depth-chart slot), and report both beside the binary threshold.\nASSUMPTION: The 50 PA / 20 IP threshold is arbitrary and treats a 20 IP reliever and a 50 PA bench bat as equal. Pitchers and hitters likely have very different base rates, so a pooled top-50 may be mostly one group depending on the ranking.\nIDEA: Rank and evaluate hitters and pitchers separately, or fix a quota (e.g., 25/25), and run a sensitivity analysis on thresholds fixed before the holdout.\nASSUMPTION: Hindsight risk in the 2025 holdout. The design was written 2026-10-09 after the 2026 season ended and after the 2025 cohort pool size was seen. Anyone who knows 2026 outcomes, including the authors from general baseball knowledge, could shape features, baselines, or k. The statement \"not inspected for outcomes\" is unverified and is not independently checkable.\nIDEA: Pre-register by hashing the frozen code and config before retrieving any 2026 outcomes, and prefer a purely historical blinded check (e.g., rerun on 2018 to 2024 with rolling-origin splits) so the conclusion does not rest on one cohort.\nASSUMPTION: Leakage risk through \"highest level reached in Y\" and \"MLB playing time in Y\". Transactions dated through Dec 31 mean the feature window includes events after election (e.g., minor-league deals signed in December), and the elected free agency event type itself may leak information (e.g., players who elect after being outrighted have different histories than players released). Age at Dec 31 and as-of dates must be checked per event.\nUNKNOWN: availability_time_of_each_feature_relative_to_decision_date\nASSUMPTION: The multi-cohort split is nearly a one-sample test. Only one holdout cohort of about 839 players exists, and the same players recur across cohorts (acknowledged, not quantified). The 2019 to 2020 cohort with a 60-game scaled threshold and the missing 2020 cohort leave only about five clean training years, with a COVID shift in roster behavior.\nUNKNOWN: outcome_positive_base_rate_per_cohort\nUNKNOWN: number_of_holdout_players_also_in_training_cohorts\nASSUMPTION: Base rate risk cuts both ways. If the base rate is around 10 to 20 percent of about 800 players, the top-50 count has a standard error of a few players, so a +3 threshold is about one standard deviation of sampling noise from a single cohort. A bootstrap on one cohort captures player sampling but not season-to-season variation (injury luck, club needs), so the interval is too narrow for generalization.\nCALC: 839 * 0.15 = 125.85\nASSUMPTION: The base rate of 0.15 in the CALC above is an illustrative placeholder, not a measured value. The stopping rule (<15 positives per cohort) is so lax that it will almost never trigger and does not guarantee the top-50 comparison is informative.\nASSUMPTION: k=50 is justified only by an unchecked statement about club signing volume. Clubs sign minor-league deals for many reasons (org depth for affiliates) that don't aim at MLB contribution. The +3 hits threshold has a rationale (\"scout-level noise\") with no data. A top-50 hit gain of 3 on a base of maybe 20 hits is roughly 15 percent relative; on a base of 10 hits, 30 percent. Neither is tied to wins or cost.\nUNKNOWN: club_actual_minor_league_contract_and_NRI_volume_at_k\nIDEA: Replace the fixed +3 with a threshold derived from a decision model: value of an additional usable depth player in runs or dollars versus scout time, and report results for several k (25, 50, 100) with a fixed ordering of primary versus secondary before the holdout.\nASSUMPTION: Baselines B0 and B1 may be weak or unfair in either direction. B0 uses MLB playing time and is a strong proxy for the outcome, since the same players tend to get MLB time again. B1 uses OPS and K-BB% across levels without park, league, or age-relative-to-level adjustments, so it is a straw man for hitters at Triple-A versus Double-A. M gets to learn age-by-level interactions via regression while the baselines have none. Choosing the better baseline on a single validation cohort (2024) is itself noisy.\nIDEA: Add stronger comparators: persistence (Y+1 outcome equals Y outcome), a plain age-and-level-only model, and any public projection systems (e.g., public aggregated projections for MLB-experienced players), and test M against the best of these on the holdout.\nUNKNOWN: existing_public_tools_covering_minor_league_free_agent_ranking\nASSUMPTION: Existing public resources already approximate this: public transaction trackers and free-agent lists, prospect and minor-league leaderboards, public projection systems, and club-agnostic fan analyses of minor-league free agents. I have not verified which are current or how good they are. If these already give the same ranking, the project gives parity, not an edge, and clubs with private tracking data and scouting reports start from a better information set.\nASSUMPTION: What would make this LOOK useful while providing little advantage: (1) M beats B0/B1 mainly because of MLB-time and level features that any scout reads off a transactions page; (2) the binary outcome rewards opportunity, not talent; (3) a clean-looking bootstrap interval from one cohort; (4) the Cubs retrospective compares to signings already made without knowing alternatives, offers, or competing bids; (5) high AUROC across a pool where most players are trivially low-probability; (6) retrospective examples of \"players the Cubs missed\" selected after outcomes are known; (7) no cost or availability constraint.\nIDEA: Report AUROC and precision restricted to the plausibly actionable segment (players with no MLB service-time lock, not already re-signed within a few days), plus a \"what did M add beyond B0 and public projections\" incremental-value test, and require the lift to survive in the \"no MLB in Y\" segment, where the claimed edge for neglected players should live.\nASSUMPTION: The feasibility evidence covers only event counts and API availability. It does not show that features such as season-Y stats by level, Y+1 outcomes, or as-of timing are obtainable within the stated source terms (individual, non-commercial, non-bulk use) or the 3 remaining sessions. The design's reliance on bulk cohort construction may conflict with those terms.\nCONFLICT: source_terms_vs_bulk_cohort_construction\nUNKNOWN: data_acquisition_feasibility_within_source_terms_and_session_bound\nASSUMPTION: Rule 5 codes appear only from 2024, and the design's own Cubs case requires comparing against public signings that are hard to link to the DFA-type events reliably. Name matching between transaction records and stats IDs is an untested error source.\nASSUMPTION: The right verdict if only the headline holds: a positive result would show retrospective rankability, not an actionable advantage. A negative result is more informative and the design's COMPLETE_NEGATIVE rule is sound, but the design should state that a positive result alone does not justify a Cubs recommendation without availability, cost, and incremental-value tests.",
