    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/5",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "FACT",
      "text": "method_disagreement_2018_minor=530 vs 417",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/6",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "CALC",
      "text": "530 - 417 = 113",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/7",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "CONFLICT",
      "text": "milb_vs_mlb_separation_2018",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/8",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "The design avoids the S1 separation conflict by ranking the whole pool, but that makes the pool include players the decision-maker would never pursue (MLB-service-time veterans, 6-year minor-league free agents, players already agreed to re-sign). Top-50 hits may then be dominated by obvious MLB-experienced players, which B0 already ranks highly; M's lead over B0 could come from re-discovering \"had MLB time in Y\" rather than finding anything a scout would not already see.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/9",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "The November decision may not change with a ranking. Clubs already have internal lists, and the best players sign within days, often with the club that holds their rights or on terms agreed before the pool opens. The design provides no evidence that a club's actual choice set at decision time resembles the full elected-free-agency list.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/10",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "UNKNOWN",
      "text": "fraction_of_pool_that_is_actually_signable_and_available_to_a_given_club",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/11",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "The outcome (>=50 PA or >=20 IP in Y+1) measures opportunity at least as much as value. A player who signs with a team lacking depth reaches 50 PA through roster need, and a good player blocked on a deep roster does not. The design admits this bias but proposes no correction. It would also reward predicting \"who gets a 40-man spot\", which can be learned from MLB time and level alone.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/12",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "IDEA",
      "text": "Add a value-conditioned secondary outcome (e.g., WAR or runs above replacement per PA/IP, with a minimum-opportunity gate) and a opportunity-adjusted outcome (e.g., stratify by signing club's depth-chart slot), and report both beside the binary threshold.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/13",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "The 50 PA / 20 IP threshold is arbitrary and treats a 20 IP reliever and a 50 PA bench bat as equal. Pitchers and hitters likely have very different base rates, so a pooled top-50 may be mostly one group depending on the ranking.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/14",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "IDEA",
      "text": "Rank and evaluate hitters and pitchers separately, or fix a quota (e.g., 25/25), and run a sensitivity analysis on thresholds fixed before the holdout.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/15",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "Hindsight risk in the 2025 holdout. The design was written 2026-10-09 after the 2026 season ended and after the 2025 cohort pool size was seen. Anyone who knows 2026 outcomes, including the authors from general baseball knowledge, could shape features, baselines, or k. The statement \"not inspected for outcomes\" is unverified and is not independently checkable.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/16",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "IDEA",
      "text": "Pre-register by hashing the frozen code and config before retrieving any 2026 outcomes, and prefer a purely historical blinded check (e.g., rerun on 2018 to 2024 with rolling-origin splits) so the conclusion does not rest on one cohort.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/17",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "Leakage risk through \"highest level reached in Y\" and \"MLB playing time in Y\". Transactions dated through Dec 31 mean the feature window includes events after election (e.g., minor-league deals signed in December), and the elected free agency event type itself may leak information (e.g., players who elect after being outrighted have different histories than players released). Age at Dec 31 and as-of dates must be checked per event.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/18",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "UNKNOWN",
      "text": "availability_time_of_each_feature_relative_to_decision_date",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/19",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "The multi-cohort split is nearly a one-sample test. Only one holdout cohort of about 839 players exists, and the same players recur across cohorts (acknowledged, not quantified). The 2019 to 2020 cohort with a 60-game scaled threshold and the missing 2020 cohort leave only about five clean training years, with a COVID shift in roster behavior.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/20",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "UNKNOWN",
      "text": "outcome_positive_base_rate_per_cohort",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/21",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "UNKNOWN",
      "text": "number_of_holdout_players_also_in_training_cohorts",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/22",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "Base rate risk cuts both ways. If the base rate is around 10 to 20 percent of about 800 players, the top-50 count has a standard error of a few players, so a +3 threshold is about one standard deviation of sampling noise from a single cohort. A bootstrap on one cohort captures player sampling but not season-to-season variation (injury luck, club needs), so the interval is too narrow for generalization.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/23",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "CALC",
      "text": "839 * 0.15 = 125.85",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/24",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "The base rate of 0.15 in the CALC above is an illustrative placeholder, not a measured value. The stopping rule (<15 positives per cohort) is so lax that it will almost never trigger and does not guarantee the top-50 comparison is informative.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/25",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "k=50 is justified only by an unchecked statement about club signing volume. Clubs sign minor-league deals for many reasons (org depth for affiliates) that don't aim at MLB contribution. The +3 hits threshold has a rationale (\"scout-level noise\") with no data. A top-50 hit gain of 3 on a base of maybe 20 hits is roughly 15 percent relative; on a base of 10 hits, 30 percent. Neither is tied to wins or cost.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/26",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "UNKNOWN",
      "text": "club_actual_minor_league_contract_and_NRI_volume_at_k",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/27",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "IDEA",
      "text": "Replace the fixed +3 with a threshold derived from a decision model: value of an additional usable depth player in runs or dollars versus scout time, and report results for several k (25, 50, 100) with a fixed ordering of primary versus secondary before the holdout.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/28",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "Baselines B0 and B1 may be weak or unfair in either direction. B0 uses MLB playing time and is a strong proxy for the outcome, since the same players tend to get MLB time again. B1 uses OPS and K-BB% across levels without park, league, or age-relative-to-level adjustments, so it is a straw man for hitters at Triple-A versus Double-A. M gets to learn age-by-level interactions via regression while the baselines have none. Choosing the better baseline on a single validation cohort (2024) is itself noisy.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/29",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "IDEA",
      "text": "Add stronger comparators: persistence (Y+1 outcome equals Y outcome), a plain age-and-level-only model, and any public projection systems (e.g., public aggregated projections for MLB-experienced players), and test M against the best of these on the holdout.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/30",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "UNKNOWN",
      "text": "existing_public_tools_covering_minor_league_free_agent_ranking",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/31",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "Existing public resources already approximate this: public transaction trackers and free-agent lists, prospect and minor-league leaderboards, public projection systems, and club-agnostic fan analyses of minor-league free agents. I have not verified which are current or how good they are. If these already give the same ranking, the project gives parity, not an edge, and clubs with private tracking data and scouting reports start from a better information set.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/32",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "What would make this LOOK useful while providing little advantage: (1) M beats B0/B1 mainly because of MLB-time and level features that any scout reads off a transactions page; (2) the binary outcome rewards opportunity, not talent; (3) a clean-looking bootstrap interval from one cohort; (4) the Cubs retrospective compares to signings already made without knowing alternatives, offers, or competing bids; (5) high AUROC across a pool where most players are trivially low-probability; (6) retrospective examples of \"players the Cubs missed\" selected after outcomes are known; (7) no cost or availability constraint.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/33",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "IDEA",
      "text": "Report AUROC and precision restricted to the plausibly actionable segment (players with no MLB service-time lock, not already re-signed within a few days), plus a \"what did M add beyond B0 and public projections\" incremental-value test, and require the lift to survive in the \"no MLB in Y\" segment, where the claimed edge for neglected players should live.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/34",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "The feasibility evidence covers only event counts and API availability. It does not show that features such as season-Y stats by level, Y+1 outcomes, or as-of timing are obtainable within the stated source terms (individual, non-commercial, non-bulk use) or the 3 remaining sessions. The design's reliance on bulk cohort construction may conflict with those terms.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/35",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "CONFLICT",
      "text": "source_terms_vs_bulk_cohort_construction",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/36",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "UNKNOWN",
      "text": "data_acquisition_feasibility_within_source_terms_and_session_bound",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/37",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "Rule 5 codes appear only from 2024, and the design's own Cubs case requires comparing against public signings that are hard to link to the DFA-type events reliably. Name matching between transaction records and stats IDs is an untested error source.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    },
    {
      "id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1/claim/38",
      "candidate_id": "hd-20261009-131111-e450e5bb5261/candidates/0/1",
      "kind": "ASSUMPTION",
      "text": "The right verdict if only the headline holds: a positive result would show retrospective rankability, not an actionable advantage. A negative result is more informative and the design's COMPLETE_NEGATIVE rule is sound, but the design should state that a positive result alone does not justify a Cubs recommendation without availability, cost, and incremental-value tests.",
      "status": "UNVERIFIED",
      "extractor": "line_grammar/v1"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": "design-v1",
      "text": "# R001 experiment design: November free-agent pool triage (MILBFA)\n\nStatus: DRAFT v1, written 2026-10-09 before any outcome labels were inspected. Pending the early independent challenge (section 12). Changes after the challenge are recorded as v2 with reasons; this v1 stays as written.\n\n## Decision and user\n\n- User: a club's pro scouting director and the front-office staff who decide minor-league contracts and non-roster invitations (NRIs).\n- Recurring decision: every November, several hundred players elect free agency within days (S1: 585 to 908 \"elected free agency\" events per Nov-Dec window, 2018-2025). Staff must decide which few to pursue and in what order, with limited scouting attention.\n- Intervention: a ranked list of the whole pool, built only from public information available on the decision date, with each player's evidence shown.\n\n## Hypothesis (falsifiable)\n\nH1: Ranking the November pool by a simple public-data model puts more next-season MLB contributors in the top k than a naive baseline ranking does.\n\n- Population: every player with an \"elected free agency\" transaction (type DFA, description text \"elected free agency\") dated Nov 1 to Dec 31 of season Y. This avoids S1's unresolved minor-league vs MLB separation: the decision-maker sees the whole pool. A decision-time stratifier is reported separately: \"no MLB appearance in season Y\" (the low-attention segment), derived from season-Y stats.\n- Outcome (binary, defined now): the player records at least 50 MLB plate appearances or at least 20 MLB innings pitched in season Y+1, for any club. Secondary: any MLB appearance in Y+1.\n- Features: only data with event time and availability time no later than Dec 31 of season Y: age at Dec 31, highest level reached in Y, season-Y stats by level (rate stats and playing time), MLB playing time in Y and Y-1, position group (pitcher / hitter). No later transactions, no Y+1 data, no projections published after the decision date.\n\n## Baselines (simple, comparable information)\n\n- B0 (naive experience): rank by MLB playing time in season Y, then highest level reached, then younger first.\n- B1 (simple rule a scout might use): rank by highest level reached in Y, then a level-appropriate rate stat (OPS for hitters, K-BB% for pitchers), then age.\n- Model M: logistic regression on the features above, fitted on training cohorts only, preprocessing fitted on training data only. No hyperparameter search beyond a fixed, recorded regularization choice tuned on the validation cohort.\n\n## Splits and holdout\n\n- Training cohorts: 2018, 2019, 2021, 2022, 2023 (outcome seasons 2019, 2020, 2022, 2023, 2024). There is no 2020 cohort (no minor-league season in 2020). The 2019 cohort's outcome season is 2020 (shortened season): the outcome threshold is scaled by games played (60/162) and the cohort is also reported excluded as a sensitivity check.\n- Validation cohort: 2024 (outcomes 2025), used once for the fixed regularization choice and for comparing M against B0/B1 during development.\n- Final holdout: 2025 cohort (outcomes 2026, a season already complete on 2026-10-09). Not inspected for outcomes before the final evaluation. As of this writing the 2025 cohort's pool size is known from S1 (839 events), but no 2026 outcome has been retrieved or looked at.\n- Players appear in multiple cohorts; the split is by cohort year (temporal), and the same player in different years is treated as separate decisions. Reported: how many holdout players also appear in training cohorts.\n\n## Primary metric and uncertainty\n\n- Primary: number of outcome-positive players in the top k = 50 of the ranking, holdout cohort, M vs B1 (the stronger simple baseline, chosen as whichever of B0/B1 is better on validation; recorded before holdout).\n- Why k = 50: roughly the scale of minor-league contracts plus NRIs a club offers in an offseason (to be checked against public Cubs signings in the Cubs case; if the measured number differs a lot, k is re-justified before the holdout, not after).\n- Secondary: precision@25, precision@100, AUROC, calibration of M.\n- Uncertainty: paired bootstrap over players in the holdout cohort (2,000 resamples) for the difference in top-50 hits and AUROC; report 95% intervals.\n\n## Practical significance and stopping\n\n- Meaningful improvement: M finds at least 3 more outcome-positive players in its top 50 than the baseline, with the paired bootstrap interval excluding 0. Rationale: with base rates expected to be low, a few extra usable depth players per offseason is the scale at which a pro department's attention would shift; smaller differences are within scout-level noise.\n- Rejection: if M does not beat the baseline by that margin on the holdout, the usefulness claim is rejected (COMPLETE_NEGATIVE), and no Cubs recommendation is made from M.\n- Stop early (before modelling) if: outcome-positive base rate in training cohorts is so low (< 15 positives per cohort) that top-50 comparisons are not informative; record and use the pivot or stop.\n\n## What this can and cannot establish\n\n- Can: whether public decision-time data ranks the November pool better than simple rules, retrospectively.\n- Cannot: causal benefit (outcome depends on opportunity given by the signing club), adoption, wins, or comparison with clubs' private evaluations.\n- Known bias: a player's next-season MLB time partly reflects which club signed him and its roster needs.\n\n## Cubs application (dated)\n\nRetrospective case: as of 2025-12-31, apply the frozen method to the 2025 pool, list its top 50 and the \"no MLB in 2025\" segment, and compare with the minor-league contracts and NRIs the Cubs publicly signed that offseason (public transactions), then with 2026 outcomes. Report what the method would have flagged that the Cubs did not sign and vice versa, without claiming the method would have improved the Cubs' results. If H1 is rejected, the case illustrates limitations only.\n\n## Smallest implementation\n\nA CLI in cubs-edge-lab that builds the cohort tables from the existing probe cache plus the additional public queries it needs (season-Y stats and Y+1 outcomes per player), computes features with explicit as-of dates, fits M on training cohorts, evaluates M/B0/B1 with bootstrap intervals, and writes a dated ranked list for a chosen cohort. Bulk data stays local (MLBAM terms); committed outputs are aggregates and short examples.\n\n## Bounds\n\nHowlPlane sessions remaining for R001: 3 of 12 (user-raised bound). Data acquisition, build, and evaluation must fit; if not, pause and ask.\n",
      "facts": {},
      "provenance": {
        "source_type": "OPERATOR_EXPECTATION",
        "produced_by": "howl-cubs controller",
        "source_ref": "experiments/R001-MILBFA-DESIGN.md v1",
        "independent_of_implementation": true,
        "represents_observed_reality": false
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "s1-feasibility",
      "text": "Measured with the public MLB Stats API (cubs-edge-lab research/FEASIBILITY.md, merged 35f803b). 'Elected free agency' events (typeCode DFA) dated Nov 1 - Dec 31 per offseason: 2018 585, 2019 631, 2021 908, 2022 854, 2023 833, 2024 638, 2025 839. Two methods to separate minor-league from MLB elections disagree (2018: method A 530 minor / 52 MLB / 3 unresolved; method B 417 / 133 / 35). Rule 5 MLB-phase codes R5 appear only from 2024 (15 in Dec 2024). Minor-league dated game logs are returned for sampled players. Source terms: individual, non-commercial, non-bulk use only.",
      "facts": {
        "milbfa_2018_events": "585",
        "milbfa_2025_events": "839",
        "method_disagreement_2018_minor": "530 vs 417"
      },
      "provenance": {
        "source_type": "EXTERNAL_OBSERVATION",
        "produced_by": "HowlPlane S1c/S1d sessions",
        "source_ref": "https://github.com/howlcipher/cubs-edge-lab/blob/main/research/FEASIBILITY.md",
        "independent_of_implementation": true,
        "represents_observed_reality": true
      }
